<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mordvins</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mordvinians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mordvins are a Finno-Ugric people of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, divided into two main groups, the Erzya and the Moksha, each with its own language. Their forest lands bordered the Rus' principalities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> and Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> and Volga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, and they came fully under Russian rule after the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> in 1552. Mordovia is their republic within Russia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mordvins.jpg" alt="Mordvins" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vasily Vereshchagin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%92%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%89%D0%B0%D0%B3%D0%B8%D0%BD_%D0%92%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%9F%D0%B5%D1%82%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87_-_%D0%9C%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B4%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BA%D0%B8.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Mordvins is the official term used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> since 1928 for both the Erzya and the Moksha. The Mordvins do not use it as a self-designation, and when they speak of themselves as a whole they say Erzya and Moksha. The name is thought to come from an Iranian word, mard, meaning man. Possible early mentions include the Mordens in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a>' Getica and a land called Mordia in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-administrando-imperio">De Administrando Imperio</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Russian Primary Chronicle</a> names the Mordva in the 11th century.</p><p>Researchers distinguish the ancestors of the Erzya and the Moksha from the mid-1st century CE by the orientation of burials, costume and bronze jewellery. The two peoples did not intermarry before their conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The ancient Mordvins met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> around the 7th century and later paid tribute to Volga Bulgaria. In the 13th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> conquered the region and incorporated the Mordvins into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. In the late 14th century the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> took much of the western Mordvin lands, while the east was held by the Kazan Khanate. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> conquered the Kazan Khanate in 1552, all Mordvins became Russian subjects.</p><p>Under Russian rule the Mordvins suffered forced conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, displacement, migration and an influx of Russian settlers, which made them a minority in their own area. In the 17th century the Mordvins, Chuvash and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> rose against Russian rule. After the revolt was suppressed many Mordvins fled east beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, and those who stayed became serfs. Assimilation to Russian culture was rapid until a Mordvin nationalism appeared in the late 19th century. The Mordvins received an autonomous territory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1928, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a> intensified in the 1930s, and by the 1950s knowledge of their languages was declining rapidly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The two Mordvinic languages, Erzya and Moksha, belong to the Uralic family and have about 275,000 native speakers together. They are not mutually intelligible, so Russian is often used between the groups. Their literary forms were standardised in 1922 and 1923, and both are written in the Russian alphabet. Both are official languages of Mordovia alongside Russian.</p><p>The Erzya and Moksha have no common mythology. In Erzya belief the gods hatched from an egg, with Ange Patiai as the mother of gods and the sun god Chipaz after her. In Moksha belief the supreme god is Viarde Skai. Many Mordvins also live outside Mordovia, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> region and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. The Erzya now practise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and a native religion, and the Mordvins are known as skilled beekeepers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mordvins” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The population included Tatars, Chuvash, Mari, <b>Mordvins</b>, Udmurts and Bashkirs, and the tax-paying class, the qara xalıq, comprised state peasants, dependent people and prisoners of war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…centuries these groups settled in the forest-steppe around the confluence of the Volga and the Kama, in what is now Tatarstan, and established their rule over the Finnic and other peoples of the region, among them ancestors of the Mari, Udmurts and <b>Mordvins</b>, as well as the Burtas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1129 the two lands separated, and Ryazan became a principality of its own under descendants of Yaroslav Sviatoslavich, bordering the steppe and the forest lands of the <b>Mordvins</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-ryazan">Grand Principality of Ryazan</a></li></ul></section>
